{"subjectId":"java","id":"this-super","title":"this & super","group":"OOP Fundamentals","order":20,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>this</code> refers to the current object; <code>super</code> refers to the superclass part of the current object.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pointer\"></i>Uses of <code>this</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Example</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Disambiguate a field from a parameter</td><td><code>this.name = name;</code></td><td>Parameter shadows the field</td></tr><tr><td>Call another constructor</td><td><code>this(name, 0);</code></td><td>Constructor chaining — first statement only</td></tr><tr><td>Pass the current object</td><td><code>registry.add(this);</code></td><td>Callbacks, listeners</td></tr><tr><td>Return the current object</td><td><code>return this;</code></td><td>Fluent / builder APIs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Counter c = new Counter().add(5).add(10).add(-3);   // chaining via return this\n        System.out.println(c.total);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Counter {\n    int total;\n    Counter add(int total) {\n        this.total += total;   // this.total = field, total = parameter\n        return this;\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>12</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-big-up\"></i>Uses of <code>super</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Call a superclass constructor</td><td><code>super(name);</code> — first statement of the constructor</td></tr><tr><td>Call the overridden superclass method</td><td><code>super.describe()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Access a hidden superclass field</td><td><code>super.name</code> (when the subclass declares a field with the same name)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Manager m = new Manager(\"Asha\", 3);\n        System.out.println(m.describe());\n        m.showNames();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Employee {\n    protected String name;\n    String kind = \"employee\";\n    Employee(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    String describe() { return name + \" (\" + kind + \")\"; }\n}\n\nclass Manager extends Employee {\n    private final int reports;\n    String kind = \"manager\";                   // hides Employee.kind\n\n    Manager(String name, int reports) {\n        super(name);                           // must come first\n        this.reports = reports;\n    }\n\n    @Override String describe() {\n        return super.describe() + \" manages \" + reports;   // reuse parent logic\n    }\n\n    void showNames() {\n        System.out.println(this.kind + \" / \" + super.kind);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Asha (employee) manages 3\nmanager / employee</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>Employee.describe()</code> prints <em>employee</em> — fields are <strong>not</strong> polymorphic, so inside <code>Employee</code> the name <code>kind</code> always means <code>Employee.kind</code>. Avoid hiding fields; it is confusing.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-gavel\"></i>Rules</h3>\n<ul><li><code>this</code> and <code>super</code> cannot be used in a <code>static</code> context — there is no current object.</li><li>A constructor can start with <code>this(...)</code> <strong>or</strong> <code>super(...)</code>, never both.</li><li><code>super.super.method()</code> is not allowed — you can only reach one level up.</li><li>If the superclass has no no-arg constructor, every subclass constructor must call <code>super(args)</code> explicitly.</li></ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>class Animal {\n    Animal(String name) { }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    Dog() { }   // implicit super() — but Animal has no no-arg constructor\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>javac</figcaption><pre><code>Main.java:6: error: constructor Animal in class Animal cannot be applied to given types;\n    Dog() { }   // implicit super() — but Animal has no no-arg constructor\n          ^\n  required: String\n  found:    no arguments\n  reason: actual and formal argument lists differ in length</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't this be used inside a static method?</summary><div><p>A static method belongs to the class and runs without any instance, so there is no current object for <code>this</code> (or <code>super</code>) to refer to.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if you don't call super(...) in a constructor?</summary><div><p>The compiler inserts <code>super()</code> as the first statement. If the parent has no accessible no-arg constructor, compilation fails.</p></div></details></section>"}